\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Finite determinant coefficients and Newton relations}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For an endomorphism #{K} of a finite free rank-#{N} module
over #{k}, put #{e_j(K)=\trace(\Lambda^jK)}, including
#{e_0(K)=1}. Then
##{\det(t\bbI -K)=
 \sum_{j=0}^N(-1)^jt^{N-j}e_j(K).}
For #{1\le j\le N}, the integral Newton relation is
##{j e_j(K)=\sum_{\ell=1}^j(-1)^{\ell-1}
       e_{j-\ell}(K)\trace(K^\ell).}
If the image of #{j} in #{k} is a unit, multiplication by its
inverse recovers #{e_j(K)} from the preceding coefficients and
power traces.
When #{1,\ldots,N} are units, all coefficients are polynomials
in the first #{N} power traces with the indicated inverses.}

\proof{
\p{In a basis, expand the determinant by choosing either the
diagonal #{t} or a matrix entry. A choice of #{j} matrix
entries gives the sum of principal #{j}-minors with sign
#{(-1)^j}. These minors are the diagonal entries of
#{\Lambda^jK}, as in \ref{fdip-00TL}; their sum is its trace.
This proves the first finite identity.}

\p{Let #{P(t)=\det(\bbI+tK)=\sum_{j=0}^Ne_j(K)t^j}.
Polynomial differentiation of the finite determinant gives
#{P'(t)=\trace(\adjugate(\bbI+tK)K)}.
In the truncated polynomial ring #{k[t]/(t^N)}, multiplication
verifies the finite inverse
##{(\bbI+tK)^{-1}=\sum_{\ell=0}^{N-1}(-tK)^\ell.}
The adjugate identity consequently gives
##{P'(t)=P(t)\sum_{\ell=1}^N(-1)^{\ell-1}
       t^{\ell-1}\trace(K^\ell)\pmod{t^N}.}
Comparing the coefficient of #{t^{j-1}} proves Newton's
relation. Dividing by the specified unit #{j} gives the
conditional recursive version.}
}

\p{Even if #{j} is a nonunit, injectivity of multiplication by
#{j} gives uniqueness in this equation. The exterior-power
coefficient of #{K} supplies existence. For instance, let #{K} act
on the two summands of #{\mathbb Z\oplus\mathbb Z} by multiplication
by #{2} and #{3}, respectively. Its first two power traces are
#{5} and #{13}.
Hence #{2e_2=5^2-13=12}, with the unique integer solution #{e_2=6}.
In characteristic two this uniqueness can fail, as in \ref{fdip-00U0}.}
